  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nMuch quieter conditions are expected to move into the region\nbehind the very active start to the day. Mean surface flow will\nlikely remain southerly through the afternoon, which will prolong\nthe heat impacts to central Wisconsin until the evening. A few\nheat indices will likely continue to touch into the upper 90s to\nlower 100s before winds shift over to the north overnight.\n\nQuieter and much drier arrives tonight as winds pick up from the\nnorth. Gusts up to 20 to 25 mph at times will help usher in a dry\nand sunny day Tuesday as high pressure moves into the region.\nTemperatures are expected to remain near or above normal on\nTuesday before warm air advection again increases through the\nmiddle of the week. High temperatures on Thursday could get back\ntowards the middle to upper 80s and maybe even low 90s, which\ncould produce a few sporadic showers in the afternoon. Still, no\nwidespread active weather is expected until sometime Thursday\nnight into Friday with the next frontal passage. Rain and storms  \nthen stick around Friday into early Saturday before some quieter\nconditions return for the latter half of the weekend.",
